<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lady Hyegyeong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1735">1735</a>–<a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Lady Hyegyŏng, Lady Hong, Queen Heongyeong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-hyegyong">Lady Hyegyeong</a> of the Pungsan Hong clan was a Korean writer and Crown Princess of Joseon, the wife of Crown Prince Sado and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">King Jeongjo</a>. Chosen at the age of nine as the Crown Prince's bride, she married him in 1744 and bore him sons and daughters while his mental illness grew ever more violent. In 1762 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeongjo-of-joseon">King Yeongjo</a> sentenced Sado, and he died that year. Between 1795 and 1806 she wrote, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a> rather than literary Chinese, the four narratives known as The Memoirs of Lady Hyegyeong, or A Record of Sorrowful Days, recounting her life and her family's story. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">Emperor Gojong</a> gave her a posthumous title as empress in 1903.</p></div>
